/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Rotate List
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Linked List
Difficulty : Medium

Problem:
Given the head of a linked list, rotate the list to the right
by k positions.

Approach:
First, find the length of the linked list and keep track of
its last node.

Since rotating the list by its length produces the original
list, reduce k using:

    k = k % length

Then connect the last node to the head to form a circular
linked list.

The new tail is located at:

    length - k - 1

from the original head.

Break the circular connection after the new tail. The node
after it becomes the new head.

Why does this work?

When the list is rotated to the right by k positions, the last
k nodes move to the front while the first length - k nodes
move after them.

By temporarily making the list circular, we can move to the
exact position where the list must be split. Breaking the
circle at that point produces the required rotation.

Algorithm:
1. Handle empty, single-node, and zero-rotation cases.
2. Find the length and last node.
3. Reduce k using k % length.
4. Connect the last node to the head.
5. Move to the new tail at position length - k.
6. Set the node after the new tail as the new head.
7. Break the circular connection.
8. Return the new head.

Time Complexity : O(n)

Reason:
The list is traversed a constant number of times.

Space Complexity : O(1)

Reason:
Only a few pointers and integer variables are used.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* rotateRight(ListNode* head, int k) {

        // No rotation is needed for these cases.
        if (head == nullptr ||
            head->next == nullptr ||
            k == 0) {
            return head;
        }

        // Find the length and last node.
        ListNode* tail = head;
        int length = 1;

        while (tail->next != nullptr) {
            tail = tail->next;
            length++;
        }

        // Rotating by the list length gives the same list.
        k %= length;

        // No rotation remains after reducing k.
        if (k == 0) {
            return head;
        }

        // Make the linked list circular.
        tail->next = head;

        // Find the new tail.
        int stepsToNewTail = length - k;

        while (stepsToNewTail--) {
            tail = tail->next;
        }

        // The node after the new tail becomes the new head.
        ListNode* newHead = tail->next;

        // Break the circular connection.
        tail->next = nullptr;

        return newHead;
    }
};
